1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,105
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,105 --> 00:00:09,005
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:13,740
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:18,676
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:24,085
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:28,461
On the day, the book is down 176 dollars.

7
00:00:28,461 --> 00:00:32,334
BGC Group (BGC) leads, +1.7 percent.

8
00:00:32,334 --> 00:00:37,175
Atlanta Braves Holdings (BATRK) lags at -1.6.

9
00:00:37,175 --> 00:00:44,060
The checklist carries 1 item; details are in the written report.

10
00:00:44,060 --> 00:00:47,586
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

11
00:00:47,543 --> 00:00:55,192
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

12
00:00:55,192 --> 00:01:05,446
12 positions, led by Eli Lilly and, ticker LLY, Credit Acceptance, ticker CACC, Charles Schwab Corporation (The), ticker SCHW.

13
00:01:05,446 --> 00:01:16,513
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

14
00:01:16,475 --> 00:01:21,171
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

15
00:01:21,171 --> 00:01:23,519
Every morning OROS proposes.

16
00:01:23,519 --> 00:01:33,833
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

17
00:01:33,833 --> 00:01:37,691
Inside the book today: 4 positions up, 8 down.

18
00:01:37,691 --> 00:01:39,955
Best, APA at +2.89 percent.

19
00:01:39,955 --> 00:01:41,716
Worst, ABBV at -1.81.

20
00:01:41,716 --> 00:01:46,580
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

21
00:01:46,580 --> 00:01:53,540
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -8.03% per trade over 214 graded positions.

22
00:01:53,540 --> 00:02:00,416
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

23
00:02:00,416 --> 00:02:05,448
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

24
00:02:05,448 --> 00:02:17,523
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 240 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

25
00:02:17,497 --> 00:02:19,784
Over to the sports desk.

26
00:02:19,784 --> 00:02:29,219
Day 39 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

27
00:02:29,219 --> 00:02:34,175
In first place, the index fund, in gray, at 102,638.

28
00:02:34,175 --> 00:02:41,227
Then the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, 0.9 thousand back at 101,698.

29
00:02:41,227 --> 00:02:48,566
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 3.5 thousand back at 99,170.

30
00:02:48,566 --> 00:02:51,234
The race has no finish line.

31
00:02:51,234 --> 00:02:55,618
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

32
00:02:55,618 --> 00:03:00,574
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

33
00:03:00,574 --> 00:03:02,480
Nothing is restated.

34
00:03:02,480 --> 00:03:11,388
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

35
00:03:11,350 --> 00:03:18,685
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

36
00:03:18,685 --> 00:03:20,926
Nothing here is financial advice.

37
00:03:20,926 --> 00:03:23,915
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
